{"name":"Bath","hex":"#d8e9db","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Bath","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A city in Bath and North East Somerset district, Somerset, England, famous for its baths fed by a hot spring.","A village in the Netherlands."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Uncommon form of Baath."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Bath"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bathing","blocks":[{"text":"Bathing is the immersion of the body, wholly or partially, usually in water, but often in another medium such as hot air. It is most commonly practised as part of personal cleansing, and less frequently for relaxation or as a leisure activity. Cleansing the body may be solely a component of personal hygiene, but is also a spiritual part of some religious rituals. Bathing is also sometimes used medically or therapeutically, as in hydrotherapy, ice baths, or the mud bath."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Norblin_Bath_in_the_park_%28detail%29_02.jpg/330px-Norblin_Bath_in_the_park_%28detail%29_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bathing","wikidata":"Q327651"},{"title":"Bathtub","blocks":[{"text":"A bathtub, also abbreviated as bath or tub, is a container for holding water in which a person or another animal may bathe. Modern bathtubs can be made of thermoformed acrylic, porcelain-enameled steel or cast iron, or fiberglass-reinforced polyester. A bathtub is usually placed in a bathroom, either as a stand-alone fixture or in conjunction with shower plumbing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Clawfoot_bathtub.jpg/330px-Clawfoot_bathtub.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bathtub","wikidata":"Q152095"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Showering_on_Skylab_%289